\begin{abstract}
We extend the theory of hidden vanishings of the Cartier--Manin matrix,
established for the hyperelliptic family $y^2=x^d+\sigma x^r+1$, to the
superelliptic families $y^n=x^d+\sigma x^r+1$ ($n\ge2$, $\gcd(d,n)=1$) over
$\F_p$ with $\gcd(p,n)=1$. Two results are established. \textbf{Theorem~A}
constructs, with a complete proof from the axioms of the Cartier operator, the
generalised Cartier matrix with respect to the basis of differentials
$\omega_{i,k}=x^{i-1}\,dx/y^k$ ($1\le i\le g_k$): its blocks, indexed by $k=1,\dots,n-1$, are linked
by $k\mapsto k_{\mathrm{out}}\equiv k\,p^{-1}\pmod n$, with multinomial
exponent $N(k)=(k_{\mathrm{out}}\,p-k)/n$ and entries given by coefficient
extraction in $f(x)^{N(k)}$. \textbf{Theorem~B} establishes that the $p$-rank of
the Jacobian is \emph{not} the rank of this matrix after a single application,
but its \textbf{stabilised rank under iteration} (Fitting's lemma) --- a point
we first missed, which produced reproducible numerical discrepancies, and which
we correct here with a proof and a complete example. Everything is checked
independently by point counting on 18 configurations covering $n=2,3,4$ and
both regimes $p\equiv1\pmod n$ and $p\not\equiv1\pmod n$.
A further part (\S\ref{sec:blocs}--\ref{sec:classif}) proves by hand a
description of the kernel of the blocks, a duality law and a criterion for
$d=p$, and classifies $143$ cases of hidden vanishings (status
[D]/[V]/[O]); $44$ cases remain unexplained. Sections
\S\ref{sec:menger}--\ref{sec:meca} add a general Menger theorem
($\Delta<g_0\Rightarrow\det\Phi\equiv0$), its closed criterion proved for $d=p$
(checked on $6104$ cases) and the decomposition of the observed mechanisms. The tallies quoted come from distinct sweeps (different ranges of $p,d$) and are not reconciled with one another.
Version~4 adds (\S\ref{sec:stylo}--\ref{sec:remarks}) a proved degree law, two
examples that can be checked entirely by hand (one cancellation and one
Fermat-type case, $p=3$), a multiplicity/corank theorem, a survey of the
corank outside the family $p\equiv-1\pmod n$, Conjecture~G (generic support)
and a \emph{coverage correction}: the symmetry $r\leftrightarrow d-r$, which
some recent sweeps assumed, is false.
Version~4.1 (\S\ref{sec:v41}) adds: the second-iterate lemma (for
$\sigma\in\F_p$), the description of the kernel of a block as the image of a
first-order differential operator (\textbf{[D]}), a recurrence between columns
explaining the twin columns, numerical laws for the profile of the exact forms
(\textbf{[V]}), a rank bound by cuts, the census of $27$ distinct cases of exact
cancellation (instead of $33$ with duplicates) and a complete by-hand proof of
one mixed case. Version~4.2 adds a test of the variant $y^n=x^d+\sigma x^r+t$
and of values of the parameter outside $\F_p$ (\S\ref{sec:tvar}).
\end{abstract}

\section{Setting and notation}

Let $p$ be an odd prime, $n\ge2$ with $\gcd(p,n)=1$, and
$$\mathcal{C}\ :\ y^n = f(x) = x^d+\sigma x^r+1,\qquad 0<r<d,\ \gcd(d,n)=1,$$
a smooth curve over $\F_p$ (we assume $f$ has no multiple root). The condition
$\gcd(d,n)=1$ ensures total ramification (index $n$) at the unique point at
infinity $P_\infty$, which is therefore automatically $\F_p$-rational: being the
only geometric point above $x=\infty$, it is necessarily fixed by Frobenius.

By Riemann--Hurwitz (total ramification at the $d$ roots of $f$ and at
$P_\infty$, i.e.\ $d+1$ branch points), the genus is
$$g=\frac{(n-1)(d-1)}{2}.$$

The space $H^0(\mathcal{C},\Omega^1)$ has as a basis the differentials (a
classical fact for cyclic covers of $\mathbb{P}^1$; see e.g.\ Hase-Liu and
Triantafillou, \emph{Efficient Point-Counting Algorithms for Superelliptic
Curves}, arXiv:1709.02442)
$$\omega_{i,k}=\frac{x^{i-1}}{y^k}\,dx,\qquad 1\le k\le n-1,\quad 1\le i\le
g_k,\qquad g_k:=\left\lfloor\frac{kd}{n}\right\rfloor,$$
and $\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}g_k=g$.

\section{Theorem A: the generalised Cartier operator}

We recall the three properties that characterise the Cartier operator
$C:H^0(\Omega^1)\to H^0(\Omega^1)$ on a smooth curve over $\F_p$ (Cartier, 1957;
see Serre, \emph{Sur la topologie des vari\'et\'es alg\'ebriques en
caract\'eristique $p$}): for every function $h$ of the function field and every
integer $s$,
$$\text{(i) } C(h^p\,\omega)=h\,C(\omega);\qquad
\text{(ii) } C(x^s\,dx)=0 \text{ if } s\not\equiv p-1\!\!\pmod p;\qquad
\text{(iii) } C(x^{p-1}\,dx)=dx.$$
These three axioms determine $C$ uniquely on every subspace spanned by
differentials of the form $g(x)\,dx$ with $g\in\F_p(x)$: writing
$g(x)=\sum_m c_m x^m$, additivity and (i) applied to $h=x^{j-1}$, with
$x^m\,dx=(x^{j-1})^p\cdot x^{p-1}dx$ for $m=pj-1$, give the classical formula
\begin{equation}
C\Big(\sum_m c_m x^m\,dx\Big)=\sum_{j\ge1} c_{pj-1}^{1/p}\,x^{j-1}\,dx. \tag{$\star$}
\end{equation}

\begin{lemma}[Inversion of $y^k$]
\label{lemma:inversion}
For $1\le k\le n-1$, let $k_{\mathrm{out}}\in\{1,\dots,n-1\}$ be the unique
integer with $k_{\mathrm{out}}\,p\equiv k\pmod n$, and set
$$N(k)=\frac{k_{\mathrm{out}}\,p-k}{n}\ \in\ \mathbb{Z}_{\ge0}.$$
Then, in the function field of $\mathcal{C}$,
$$\frac{1}{y^k}=f(x)^{N(k)}\cdot\Big(\frac{1}{y^{k_{\mathrm{out}}}}\Big)^{p}.$$
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
By construction $k_{\mathrm{out}}\,p-k=nN(k)$, so $y^{nN(k)}=y^{k_{\mathrm{out}}p-k}$,
i.e.\ $y^{nN(k)}\cdot y^k=y^{k_{\mathrm{out}}p}$. Since $y^n=f(x)$, the left-hand
side equals $f(x)^{N(k)}\cdot y^k$, hence
$f(x)^{N(k)}\cdot y^k=y^{k_{\mathrm{out}}p}=(y^{k_{\mathrm{out}}})^p$. Dividing
both sides by $y^k\cdot(y^{k_{\mathrm{out}}})^p$ gives the claimed equality.
($k_{\mathrm{out}}$ exists and is unique because $p$ is invertible modulo $n$ by
the hypothesis $\gcd(p,n)=1$; $N(k)\ge0$ because $k_{\mathrm{out}}\ge1$, so $k_{\mathrm{out}}p\ge p\ge k$ as soon as $p\ge n$, a condition satisfied in all our uses; $N(k)<p$ because $k_{\mathrm{out}}<n$.)
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Generalised Cartier operator]
\label{thm:A}
For $1\le k\le n-1$ and $1\le i\le g_k$,
$$C(\omega_{i,k})=\sum_{i'=1}^{g_{k_{\mathrm{out}}}}
\Big[\,\text{coeff.\ of } x^{\,p\,i'-i}\text{ in } f(x)^{N(k)}\,\Big]\;
x^{i'-1}\,\frac{dx}{y^{k_{\mathrm{out}}}}.$$
In other words, the matrix of $C$ in the basis $(\omega_{i,k})$ has, at row
$(k_{\mathrm{out}},i')$ and column $(k,i)$, the coefficient of $x^{pi'-i}$ in the
trinomial expansion of $f(x)^{N(k)}=(x^d+\sigma x^r+1)^{N(k)}$ --- extracted, as
in the classical case $n=2$, by the multinomial formula
$$[\,x^{m}\,]\,f(x)^{N}=\sum_{\substack{\alpha+\beta+\gamma=N\\ d\alpha+r\beta=m}}
\binom{N}{\alpha,\beta,\gamma}\,\sigma^{\beta}.$$
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lemma:inversion}, $\omega_{i,k}=x^{i-1}\,f(x)^{N(k)}\cdot
\big(y^{-k_{\mathrm{out}}}\big)^p\,dx$. Put $h=y^{-k_{\mathrm{out}}}$ and
$\omega'=x^{i-1} f(x)^{N(k)}\,dx$: then $\omega_{i,k}=h^p\,\omega'$, and by axiom
(i), $C(\omega_{i,k})=h\cdot C(\omega')$. Now $\omega'=g(x)\,dx$ with
$g(x)=x^{i-1} f(x)^{N(k)}\in\F_p[x]$, so ($\star$) applies: writing
$g(x)=\sum_m c_m x^m$,
$$C(\omega')=\sum_{j\ge1}c_{pj-1}^{1/p}\,x^{j-1}\,dx.$$
Since $c_m\in\F_p$ satisfies $c_m^{1/p}=c_m$ (Fermat's little theorem: we work
over $\F_p$ exactly, not over an extension), we obtain
$$C(\omega_{i,k})=h\cdot\sum_{j\ge1}c_{pj-1}^{1/p}\,x^{j-1}\,dx
=\sum_{j\ge1}\big[\,\text{coeff.\ of }x^{pj-1}\text{ in } x^{i-1}f(x)^{N(k)}\,\big]\,
x^{j-1}\,\frac{dx}{y^{k_{\mathrm{out}}}}.$$
The coefficient of $x^{pj-1}$ in $x^{i-1}f(x)^{N(k)}$ is that of $x^{pj-i}$ in
$f(x)^{N(k)}$. Putting $i'=j$, and observing that $C(\omega_{i,k})\in
H^0(\Omega^1)$ necessarily decomposes on the basis
$(x^{i'-1}dx/y^{k_{\mathrm{out}}})_{1\le i'\le g_{k_{\mathrm{out}}}}$ (the Cartier
operator preserves holomorphy, a classical fact), the sum restricts to
$1\le i'\le g_{k_{\mathrm{out}}}$, which gives the statement.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Terminology]
Strictly speaking, the matrix $M$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:A} is the matrix of the
\textbf{Cartier} operator, sometimes distinguished in the literature from the
\textbf{Hasse--Witt} matrix (which is its adjoint/transpose relative to Serre
duality); see Achter--Howe, \emph{Hasse--Witt and Cartier--Manin matrices: a
warning and a request}, arXiv:1710.10726, for a warning about this recurring
confusion. The distinction has no bearing on any statement below: the rank (raw
or stabilised) is invariant under transposition.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
For $n=2$ the unique block $k=1$ satisfies $k_{\mathrm{out}}=1$ (since
$1\cdot p\equiv1\pmod2$ for every odd $p$) and $N(1)=(p-1)/2$: we recover exactly
the classical Cartier--Manin matrix. For $p\equiv1\pmod n$, $p^{-1}\equiv1\pmod n$
so $k_{\mathrm{out}}=k$ for all $k$: the matrix is block diagonal (each block
closes on itself). For $p\not\equiv1\pmod n$ the blocks are permuted along the
orbits of $k\mapsto k\,p^{-1}\bmod n$; Theorem~\ref{thm:A}, written with the full
$g\times g$ matrix (and not block by block), covers this case without
modification.
\end{remark}

\section{Theorem B: the $p$-rank is the stabilised rank, not the raw rank}

This is the point we first missed. The construction of Theorem~\ref{thm:A} has
been correct from the start; the error, identified only after a systematic
comparison with an independent point count, was to read the $p$-rank as
$\rg(M)$ instead of the stabilised rank under iteration.

\begin{lemma}[Rank stabilisation -- Fitting]
\label{lemma:fitting}
Let $T:V\to V$ be an $\F_p$-linear endomorphism of a finite-dimensional vector
space of dimension $g$. The sequence $(\rg(T^m))_{m\ge1}$ is non-increasing and
stabilises for $m\ge g$: there is $N\le g$ with
$\rg(T^N)=\rg(T^{N+1})=\cdots$, and for this $N$,
$$V=\Ker(T^N)\oplus\operatorname{Im}(T^N),$$
with $T$ bijective on $\operatorname{Im}(T^N)$ and nilpotent on $\Ker(T^N)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$\operatorname{Im}(T^{m+1})=T(\operatorname{Im}(T^m))\subseteq\operatorname{Im}(T^m)$
gives the (weak) decrease of the dimensions. A non-increasing sequence of
integers in $\{0,\dots,g\}$ cannot decrease strictly more than $g$ times, so it
stabilises at some index $N\le g$. For such an $N$, $\rg(T^N)=\rg(T^{2N})$ implies
$\operatorname{Im}(T^N)=\operatorname{Im}(T^{2N})=T^N(\operatorname{Im}(T^N))$, so
$T^N$ (hence $T$) is surjective, hence bijective, on $\operatorname{Im}(T^N)$:
this is the classical Fitting lemma. The restriction of $T$ to $\Ker(T^N)$ is
nilpotent by definition of $\Ker(T^N)$, and $V=\Ker(T^N)\oplus\operatorname{Im}(T^N)$
follows from the rank--nullity theorem applied to $T^N$ together with
$\Ker(T^N)\cap\operatorname{Im}(T^N)=0$ (if $v\in\operatorname{Im}(T^N)\cap\Ker(T^N)$,
write $v=T^Nw$; since $T^N$ is bijective on $\operatorname{Im}(T^N)$ and
$T^N v=0$, we get $v=0$).
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$p$-rank = stabilised rank of the Cartier matrix]
\label{thm:B}
Let $M(\sigma)$ be the matrix of the generalised Cartier operator
(Theorem~\ref{thm:A}) evaluated at a point $\sigma\in\F_p^\times$. The $p$-rank
of $\mathrm{Jac}(\mathcal{C}_\sigma)$ equals the stabilised rank $\rg(M(\sigma)^N)$
of Lemma~\ref{lemma:fitting} (for $N\le g$ such that the sequence has
stabilised), \textbf{and not} necessarily $\rg(M(\sigma))$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Justification]
The $p$-rank of $\mathrm{Jac}(\mathcal{C})$ is, by definition, the dimension of
the \'etale part of $\mathrm{Jac}(\mathcal{C})[p]$; via the Dieudonn\'e module
theory of $\mathrm{Jac}(\mathcal{C})[p]$ (identified with
$H^1_{\mathrm{dR}}(\mathcal{C}/\F_p)$ equipped with the Frobenius $F$ and
Verschiebung $V$ operators, cf.\ Oda, \emph{The first de Rham cohomology group
and Dieudonn\'e modules}, Ann.\ Sci.\ ENS 1969), it is the dimension of the
maximal subspace on which $F$ (equivalently, by Serre duality, the Cartier
operator $C=V$ on $H^0(\Omega^1)$) acts \textbf{bijectively} --- that is, exactly
$\dim\operatorname{Im}(M^N)$ in the Fitting decomposition of
Lemma~\ref{lemma:fitting}, and not $\rg(M)$ in general. The two coincide if and
only if $M$ has no non-trivial Jordan block at eigenvalue $0$, which is not
guaranteed a priori for a block matrix such as that of Theorem~\ref{thm:A} when
$n\ge3$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
We do not claim to reprove here Oda's identification of $H^1_{\mathrm{dR}}$ with
the Dieudonn\'e module: it is a classical theorem that we cite, exactly as the
case $n=2$ relies, without reproving it, on the classical results relating the
$p$-rank to the Cartier--Manin matrix (Manin for the Hasse--Witt matrix; Yui for
the product $M\,M^{(p)}\cdots M^{(p^{g-1})}$ over $\F_q$, whose rank is the $p$-rank;
over $\F_p$ this product is $M^g$). The rank of $M$ alone only gives $g$ minus the
$a$-number. What we establish
ourselves, with a complete proof (Lemma~\ref{lemma:fitting}), is that the
\emph{``rank''} to take in this classical statement is the stabilised rank under
iteration, not the raw rank --- a distinction invisible in all the hyperelliptic
$n=2$ cases tested in this project (where stabilisation always occurred at the
first iteration, though we cannot assert here that this is always so), but which
shows up from $n=3$ on.
\end{remark}

\section{A complete worked example}

Take $n=3$, $d=4$, $r=1$, $p=7$. Then $g=(n-1)(d-1)/2=3$, with
$g_1=\lfloor4/3\rfloor=1$ and $g_2=\lfloor8/3\rfloor=2$. Since
$p=7\equiv1\pmod3$, the blocks close on themselves ($k_{\mathrm{out}}=k$), with
$N(1)=2$ and $N(2)=4$.

\paragraph{Case $\sigma=1$.} The block $k=1$ is $1\times1$ and zero (no edge in
the support). The block $k=2$ is
$M^{(2)}=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}5&5\\4&4\end{smallmatrix}\right)\pmod7$
(direct computation by the formula of Theorem~\ref{thm:A}, with $f=1+x+x^4$ and
$N(2)=4$), of rank $1$. The sequence of iterated ranks is $(1,1,1,\dots)$:
stable from the start. Total
stabilised rank $=0+1=1$. \emph{Independent check} (point count over $\F_{7^m}$,
$m=1,\dots,6$, polynomial $L$ reconstructed by the Newton identities): true
$p$-rank $=1$. \textbf{Exact agreement.}

\paragraph{Case $\sigma=3$.} The block $k=2$ is (still by the formula of
Theorem~\ref{thm:A})
$M^{(2)}=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}3&1\\5&4\end{smallmatrix}\right)\pmod7$, of rank
$1$ -- \textbf{but} $\big(M^{(2)}\big)^2=0\pmod7$: the sequence of iterated
ranks is $(1,0,0,\dots)$. Total stabilised rank $=0$ (the block $k=1$ is $[0]$). \emph{Independent check}: true
$p$-rank $=0$. \textbf{Exact agreement} -- whereas the \emph{non-stabilised} rank
$\rg(M)=1$ would have been wrong.
\begin{remark}
This second case is precisely the one where a naive reading of the rank (without
iteration) produces a wrong result -- and it is by confronting this error with
an independent point count that Theorem~\ref{thm:B} was isolated.
\end{remark}

\section{Independent verification}

The two theorems above were tested, for each configuration $(p,n,d,r,\sigma)$,
against a \textbf{second, entirely independent method}: direct point counting of
$\mathcal{C}_\sigma$ over $\F_{p^m}$ for $m=1,\dots,2g$ (finite fields built via
discrete logarithms, without using Cartier theory), reconstruction of the
polynomial $L(T)=\sum c_kT^k$ by the Newton identities from the power sums
$s_m=p^m+1-\#\mathcal{C}(\F_{p^m})$ (exact integer arithmetic, checked to be
integral at each step), then reading off the $p$-rank on the $p$-adic Newton
polygon of $L(T)$ (length of the slope-zero segment).

