\section{Outside the family $p\equiv-1\pmod n$: the corank}
\label{sec:corang}

\S\ref{sec:meca} records corank $1$ in the $91$ cases of the family
$p\equiv-1\pmod n$. For a $2$-cycle it suffices that $p^2\equiv1\pmod n$; the
complementary family $p\not\equiv\pm1\pmod n$ behaves differently.

\begin{theorem}[\textbf{[D]} multiplicity and corank]
\label{thm:ordcor}
Let $\Phi(s)\in M_g(\F[s])$ ($\F$ a field) and $s_0\in\F$ with
$\dim\Ker\Phi(s_0)=c$. Then $(s-s_0)^c$ divides $\det\Phi(s)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $P\in GL_g(\F)$ whose last $c$ columns span $\Ker\Phi(s_0)$. The last $c$
columns of $\Phi(s)P$ are $\bigl(\Phi(s)-\Phi(s_0)\bigr)$ applied to these
vectors; their entries are divisible by $s-s_0$. By multilinearity of the
determinant in these $c$ columns, $(s-s_0)^c$ divides $\det(\Phi P)=\det\Phi\cdot\det P$,
and $\det P\in\F^\times$. \end{proof}

Consequence: if $\det\Phi\not\equiv0$ vanishes at $\sigma_0$ with a corank $c$
there, its multiplicity there is $\ge c$. This is what is observed: in
$(12,5,13,1,7)$, $g_0=7$, $g_1=11$, we find (\textbf{[V]})
$\det\Phi=2\sigma^7(\sigma^4-1)^2$ in $\F_5[\sigma]$ and a corank $2$ for every
$\sigma\in\F_5^\times$; the neighbouring case $(12,5,13,6,7)$ has corank $1$.

\paragraph{Survey \textbf{[V]}.} For $p^2\equiv1\pmod n$, $p\not\equiv\pm1$, with
$p\le31$, $n\le40$, $d\le30$, $g_0\le12$, all $r$, deduplicated by
